---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { GLYPHS, renderGlyph } from '../lib/script';

const groups = [
  { key: 'vowel', title: 'Vowels' },
  { key: 'digraph', title: 'Sound pairs' },
  { key: 'consonant', title: 'Consonants' },
  { key: 'mark', title: 'Marks' },
] as const;
---

<Base title="Hutt script" description="The Hutt script alphabet with pronunciation guide and a live transliterator.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>Hutt script</h1>
    <p>
      Huttese is written with one glyph per sound. Words sit on a shared baseline, like letters carved into a Mos Espa sign. Type anything below to see it
      written out.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section transliterate">
    <label for="tx"><b>Transliterator</b></label>
    <textarea id="tx" spellcheck="false">bo shuda, pateesa!</textarea>
    <div class="out" data-out aria-live="polite"></div>
  </section>

  {
    groups.map((gr) => (
      <section class="section">
        <h2>{gr.title}</h2>
        <div class="glyph-grid">
          {GLYPHS.filter((g) => g.group === gr.key).map((g) => (
            <div class="glyph-card">
              <Fragment set:html={renderGlyph(g.key, 60)} />
              <b>{g.key}</b>
              {g.ipa && <span class="ipa">/{g.ipa}/</span>}
              <small>{g.sound}</small>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <section class="section prose">
    <h2>Pronunciation guide</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Vowels are pure</strong>, like Spanish or Quechua – the language Ben Burtt drew on when inventing Huttese. <em>a</em> = “ah”, <em>e</em> = “eh”, <em>i</em> and <em>ee</em> = “ee”, <em>o</em> = “oh”, <em>u</em> and <em>oo</em> = “oo”.</li>
      <li><strong>Stress</strong> usually falls on the second-to-last syllable: pa-<b>TEE</b>-sa, wu-pi-<b>U</b>-pi. Respellings in the course show the stressed syllable in CAPITALS.</li>
      <li><strong>The apostrophe</strong> (as in <em>k'wanna</em>, <em>h'chu</em>) is a tiny catch – a short “uh” between consonants.</li>
      <li><strong>The x</strong> in <em>ap-xmasi</em> is traditionally a wet lip-smack. Be as disgusting as you dare.</li>
      <li><strong>Speak from the belly.</strong> Hutts are enormous; Jabba’s voice was pitched down in post-production. The course audio does the same.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      About this script: the films show an in-universe alphabet on Tatooine signs and podracer displays, but its letterforms were never fully published.
      The glyphs here are an original design made for this course, following the same one-symbol-per-letter idea.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script>
    import { renderScript } from '../lib/script';
    const tx = document.querySelector<HTMLTextAreaElement>('#tx')!;
    const out = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-out]')!;
    const draw = () => (out.innerHTML = renderScript(tx.value, { size: 36 }));
    tx.addEventListener('input', draw);
    draw();
  </script>
</Base>
